[Music]  savine thank you so much for joining me  so what's gone wrong with physics so  physics  is a very large discipline that has a  lot of sub  most of which are going perfectly fine  you know we're talking about stuff like  um material science or plasma physics  or optics quantum optics and so on so  these are not the areas that i'm  concerned with i'm talking specifically  about the foundations of physics  that's the part where we deal with the  really big  questions like what is space what is  time what is meta made of  and so on and in these areas we just  haven't made progress since the 1970s  uh which brings up the question well why  so so one answer that for instance like  to give is that  uh we're just facing very very difficult  questions right and it takes a lot of  time to figure out uh  what's really going on um that's  certainly part of the story but it  doesn't really explain why  why the physicists in these disciplines  have  come up with so many theories that all  turned out to be wrong  um and i think that requires explanation  and  the explanation that i think is the most  plausible one  is that they are just not learning from  their mistakes so they're using very  specific  methods to develop new theories  and they're just not working and  specifically they  are using very restrictive notions of  mathematical beauty  so they construct theories that have to  comply with these ideas of mathematical  beauty and it's just not working  and so that's my answer to the question  what's what's going wrong with physics  could we talk about a couple of theories  which are two  based on outdated ideas of mathematical  plotting  this idea that um the fundamental forces  have to be unified  so that there is one big unified force  so we currently deal with  four different fundamental forces that's  the  electromagnetic force the strong and the  weak nuclear force and then there's  gravity  and physicists speak of a grand unified  force  if you combine everything except for  the gravitational force and there are  specific theories for that  they're called grand unified theories  and people have  you know tried to find evidence for that  since um the 1980s  uh instead they have ruled out uh lots  of these ideas  but physicists haven't given up on the  idea it's just too beautiful  you know um so this is one of these  ideas uh then there is  um supersymmetry um which has also been  around since the 1980s  people started uh looking for this  already with the large  electron positron collider and then with  the tavatron  in the united states and um now with uh  the lhc  and they haven't found it over and over  again but they  they they're not giving up on the idea  because it's just too it's just too  beautiful i suppose  um and so i just think that this is not  a good strategy uh to proceed it doesn't  lead us anywhere  um you have to learn from the results  that you get that's how science works  so what does a messy approach to physics  prioritize where does the funding go for  you what what are you looking at  what am i looking at specifically so  yeah so so i i should put ahead that i'm  always very careful if people  ask me that because of course i have a  personal opinion what we should be doing  but really my point is not that everyone  should be doing what i think is great  and the problem is that we're not having  a discussion about what is going  wrong so i think this community needs to  have a hard  look at itself and just think  more about the question where to best  put the money  but since you're asking me in particular  unsurprisingly i think  there should be more money invested in  exactly the stuff that i work on  so that's uh for one um that's  modifications of gravity so that's an  alternative explanation for  um dark matter so dark matter  is a hypothesis that physicists have  come up with to explain some puzzling  things we see out in the cosmos  that i can talk about in great detail  but it may take  take me several hours um and so  um the the idea that there is a certain  type of matter that we just have not  been able to produce and detect on earth  is one way  um to explain these observations um the  alternative  is that there's something about gravity  that we're misunderstanding so we  need a better theory of gravity and  that's what's called modified gravity so  this is one of the things that i  um work on which i think is very um  promising um so people have  looked for these supposed particles that  make up dark matter but none of them  have been found  uh and it's looking less and less and  appealing  every year there are more null results  so i think that it's  a natural point in time where more  effort  should be made to look for modified  gravity  and there is just on the theoretical  side there has been very little work  because  uh most people have focused on this  particle dark matter  idea so so that big gaps in our  understanding  um just exactly what for example you  should look for experimentally  how could you even tell these things  apart what you know what experiments  should you even make and and so there  you need some uh theoretical  investigation of that to even know  what what exactly you are looking for  and  the other um research area that i've  become increasingly interested in are  the foundations of quantum mechanics  um so there is um an assumption  in um one of the most important theorems  in that area which is called the free  will assumption  um and i think this assumption is just  wrong and it has to go out of the window  and it's really standing in the way of  progress  um it's why we have not been able to  make sense of quantum mechanics  you know there's this debate that has  been going on for  a century um how do we understand  quantum mechanics what does he mean  you know what does it say about the role  of the observer does human consciousness  play a role in the measurement process  and all that kind of stuff and i think  it's this this free will assumption  is standing in the way can you expand on  that  uh yes so i'm talking specifically about  something that's called bell's  theorem uh belt theorem plays  a very essential role in quantum  mechanics  because it tells you that  if you are trying to replace quantum  mechanics  with a theory that works the same way  that  all pre-quantum theory worked like a  newtonian mechanics basically  where you have a deterministic evolution  uh given by some differential equation  that works on an initial state okay so  that's a little bit of mathematics  but the point is that these pre-quantum  theories have a particular mathematical  structure that quantum mechanics just  has not in quantum mechanics you have  this random element  so in quantum mechanics the the  randomness that you have is not because  you're missing some information  the way that it would be the case if you  throw dice  for example but the randomness in  quantum mechanics is supposedly  fundamental it's not because you're  lacking information it's just  the way the universe is and so  belt's theorem tells you that you cannot  replace this randomness in quantum  mechanics with a lack of information  this is what is called a hidden  variables theory  where you say well quantum mechanics is  just not the end of the story  but the reason that it looks like  there's something random happening is  that we're just  you know we're missing something there's  a deeper underlying structure  uh where there is this information that  if we had it would actually tell us  exactly what the measurement outcome is  and so that's the relevance  uh of bell's theorem and now um the  so maybe uh let me tell you more  precisely what the theorem says  so uh it says that if you have such a  hidden variable theory  um then uh in a certain type of  experiment  that correlations that have to obey an  inequality  that spells inequality but you can  experimentally show that this inequality  is violated  and so the conclusion from all that is  that  you rule out these hidden variables um  ideas and so um that's why  people think that we have to accept that  nature is just not deterministic and  that it's not local in the way that  einstein wanted it and so on  but and here come back to this issue  with the free will assumption  um this theorem um as any mathematical  theorem  requires a list of assumptions one of  which is this free will assumption which  basically says  that the experimenter is free to choose  the detector setting whichever way they  like so that's how it's usually  interpreted  and the problem with that is is um i  mean on the face of it it's just  inconsistent you know you're basically  postulating that the theory is not  deterministic and then you use the  argument from birth theorem to conclude  that it's not deterministic which makes  absolutely no sense  um so i've been investigating the  question what happens  if you throw out this free will  assumption in which case  belt theorem just does not follow so  this idea  is not a new it's something that worse  um  you know people have been thinking about  already in the 1950s it goes under the  name  super determinism um and  for a long time people have thought that  yes it's technically an option like  mathematically it's there  of course you can just throw out this  assumption but  it's unscientific uh because such a  theory  would never be predictive um that was  the idea so people called it a  conspiracy theory  because um if you wanted to make any  prediction with  with such a theory then you would have  to put  all the information about the  measurement outcome uh  into the theory already so you can't  predict anything with it  and if that was the case it would indeed  be non-scientific but the the thing is  you don't have to do that so that this  argument is just  wrong and um you know basically just  last week i finished  a calculation with my postdoc that shows  an explicit counter example  to this claim so super determinism is  definitely not a conspiracy theory or  unscientific in the sense that people  have thought  for various decades and i think that's a  very that's a very important result  because it opens the doors  to replacing quantum theory with a more  fundamental with a better theory  in which you could actually calculate  the outcome of a quantum measurement  which  um most physicists believe today is just  not possible but you have to do  dedicated experiments uh to actually get  there  and so i think that's very important  because  in all these fancy technological devices  uh  including the ones that we just  communicate with  our ultimately based on quantum  mechanics they wouldn't work without  quantum mechanics  so certainly if we were able to replace  this theory with a better theory that's  more predictive  that allows us to calculate new effects  allows us to do new things  that would be super important um also  for technological  applications so i'm a theorist myself  you know i come more from this  uh the question like how does the  universe work can i describe it with  mathematics  but of course it has this um  technological aspect to it  could you tell us any more about the  calculation that you finished in the  last week with your postdoc or is that  something that needs to await  and if if not let's talk about the  technological application well i i  i'm hoping that so we're in the final  round of checking the calculations i  just hope that nothing dramatically goes  wrong there  um so um the the idea is that  um this is a very simple example  is what was called a toy model um in in  the literature  um that demonstrates explicitly that you  can have  a super deterministic model that exactly  reproduces the predictions of quantum  mechanics but does not require any fine  tuning  now the thing about this this toy model  is that it's not any better than quantum  mechanics because the only thing it does  for you is that it reproduces exactly  the same thing  but it demonstrates that this argument  is wrong  that uh super determinism is necessarily  unscientific  and so that's what it's good for and  then we're we're working on a more  complicated model that would actually  allow you to do  more predictions than quantum mechanics  and we hope  um to be able to say exactly what kind  of experiment you need to make to  actually  test this and then of course i hope i'll  be able to convince someone to actually  do this experiment  you're a very skilled communicator do  you feel an obligation to communicate  science to the public as a result  yes basically um is partly because  i have for the past  let me think 10 years or so i've been  working  almost 15 15 years at research  institutes  so not at public universities so i don't  have teaching obligations  and i kind of think that  i should do something to spread the  knowledge that we have gathered  in our research and so  i use the opportunities that social  media give me  um to tell the broader public uh  what we have learned well  you've communicated it brilliantly to me  without phd so thank you very much  for more debates talks and interviews  subscribe today  to the institute of art and ideas at iai  tv  you

